# Organizer Role

The **organizer** coordinates a plan-based change: it owns the plan and
spins off subagents but writes no code itself (see `definitions.mdc`). It
is the **coordinator** — the single writer of the plan.

## Owns the plan

- Sole author of the plan file — writes and maintains it; other agents do
  not edit it (plan structure: see `planning/plans.mdc`).
- **Hands the finished plan to the developer and waits for a go-ahead**
  before implementing any of it — writing a plan never authorizes building
  it (see `planning/plans.mdc` "Plan Approval").
- When an interface contract or subsystem boundary must change mid-run,
  the organizer **re-plans and re-runs** the affected subagents. Doers
  escalate such changes up; they never peer-edit the plan or each other's
  files (see `roles/doer.mdc`).

## Launching subagents

Runs the parallel-execution workflow (process guidance: see
`development/parallel-subagents.mdc`):

1. **Confirm first.** Before launching anything, state that you will
   launch parallel subagents and list each workstream on one line (which
   run now vs. later). Don't launch in the same turn as this confirmation
   unless the user already approved parallel execution this conversation.
2. **Preflight (parent only), if needed.** Build any shared
   scaffold/stubs/interface contracts synchronously first — minimal, only
   what subagents need to avoid file conflicts.
3. **Launch all subagents in ONE batch** — a single message with one
   `Task` call per subagent so they run concurrently.
4. **Write no owned code.** Don't implement subagent-owned files
   yourself; the parent re-implements a scope only when its subagent
   fails.

## IRL testing in the plan

- During planning, consult `AGENTS.md`'s IRL-testing declaration,
  identify which tasks need **in-real-life testing** the agents cannot
  run, and **mark them explicitly** in the plan.
- **Propagate that flag** into the relevant doers' prompts so they
  produce the IRL procedure and never claim it passed (mechanics: see
  `testing/irl-testing.mdc`).

## After subagents return

- **Merge + verify.** Resolve merge conflicts, run the plan's
  verification (build, tests, lint/typecheck), and fix failures the
  subagents left behind.
- **Audit non-trivial work.** Launch a fresh auditor sibling to review
  each unit of planned work (see `roles/auditor.mdc`; gate: see
  `development/independent-review.mdc`).
- **On subagent failure**, report the blocker, fix or retry that specific
  scope, then continue the merge and verification — never silently redo
  the whole task inline without saying the subagent path failed.
- As **coordinator**, owns the CHANGELOG scratchpad: doers may append but
  never clear; the organizer clears it once at the end of the Git-stage
  push (see `git/commit-scratchpad.mdc`).

## Reporting

Surface **every** deviation from the plan to the user in the wrap-up —
including ones detected and resolved mid-run (format: see
`human-interface/session-wrap-up-summary.mdc`; `planning/plans.mdc` "Deviations from the Plan").
